"CSS Object Model (CSSOM) Module Level 1 (https://drafts.csswg.org/cssom/)\nciteturn1190view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"https://drafts.csswg.org/cssom/\",\"pattern\":\"To parse a CSS declaration block\"}); Total lines: 2901\nL943:   1. Set `length` to the number of items in `list`.\nL944:   2. If `index` is greater than `length`, then cite200†throw†heycam.github.io an `cite539†IndexSizeError†webidl.spec.whatwg.org ` exception.\nL945:   3. Set `new rule` to the results of performing cite540†parse a CSS rule on argument `rule`.\nL946:   4. If `new rule` is a syntax error, and `nested` is set, perform the following substeps:\nL947:      * Set `declarations` to the results of performing cite541†parse a CSS declaration block , on argument `rule`.\nL1233: ### 6.6. CSS Declaration Blocks\nL1234: \nL1235: A CSS declaration block is an ordered collection of CSS properties with their associated values, also named cite653†CSS declarations . In the DOM a cite655†CSS declaration block is a `CSSStyleDeclaration` object. A CSS declaration block has the following associated properties:\nL1236: computed flag\nL1237:     Set if the object is a computed style declaration, rather than a specified style. Unless otherwise stated it is unset.\nL1238: readonly flag\nL1239:     Set if the object is not modifiable.\nL1240: declarations\nL1241:     The cite653†CSS declarations associated with the object.\nL1242: parent CSS rule\nL1243:     The cite288†CSS rule that the cite655†CSS declaration block is associated with, if any, or null otherwise.\nL1244: owner node\nL1245:     The `cite301†Element†dom.spec.whatwg.org ` that the cite655†CSS declaration block is associated with, if any, or null otherwise.\nL1246: updating flag\nL1247:     Unset by default. Set when the cite655†CSS declaration block is updating the cite606†owner node ’s `style` attribute.\nL1248: To parse a CSS declaration block from a string `string`, follow these steps:\nL1249:   1. Let `declarations` be the returned declarations from invoking cite656†parse a list of declarations with `string`.\nL1250:   2. Let `parsed declarations` be a new empty list.\nL1251:   3. For each item `declaration` in `declarations`, follow these substeps:\nL1252:     1. Let `parsed declaration` be the result of parsing `declaration` according to the appropriate CSS specifications, dropping parts that are said to be ignored. If the whole declaration is dropped, let `parsed declaration` be null.\nL1253:     2. If `parsed declaration` is not null, append it to `parsed declarations`.\nL1254:   4. Return `parsed declarations`.\nL1255: To serialize a CSS declaration with property name `property`, value `value` and optionally an important flag set, follow these steps:\nL1256:   1. Let `s` be the empty string.\nL1257:   2. Append `property` to `s`.\nL1258:   3. Append \"`: `\" (U+003A U+0020) to `s`.\nL1259:   4. If `value` contains any non-whitespace characters, append `value` to `s`.\nL1260:   5. If the important flag is set, append \"` !important`\" (U+0020 U+0021 U+0069 U+006D U+0070 U+006F U+0072 U+0074 U+0061 U+006E U+0074) to `s`.\nL1261:   6. Append \"`;`\" (U+003B) to `s`.\nL1262:   7. Return `s`.\nL1263: Tests\nL1264: \nL1265:   * cite657†serialization-CSSDeclaration-with-important.html†wpt.fyi cite658†(live test)†wpt.live cite659†(source)†github.com L1266: To serialize a CSS declaration block `declaration block`, run the following steps. It will return a cite132†string†infra.spec.whatwg.org representing the serialization of all the declarations in `declaration block`.\nL1267: \nL1268:   1. Let `serialized props` initially be the empty cite660†list†infra.spec.whatwg.org .\nL1269: \nL1270:   2. Let `already serialized` initially be the empty cite661†set .\nL1271: \nL1272:   3. Let `decls` be `declaration block`’s cite602†declarations .\nL1273: \nL1274:   4. For each cite653†CSS declaration `decl` in `decls`:\nL1325: A cite655†CSS declaration block has these cite694†attribute change steps†dom.spec.whatwg.org for its cite606†owner node with `localName`, `value`, and `namespace`:\nL1326:   1. If the cite600†computed flag is set, then return.\nL1327:   2. If the cite695†updating flag is set, then return.\nL1328:   3. If `localName` is not \"`style`\", or `namespace` is not null, then return.\nL1329:   4. If `value` is null, empty the cite602†declarations .\nL1330:   5. Otherwise, let the cite602†declarations be the result of cite541†parse a CSS declaration block from a string `value`.\nL1331: \nL1332: When a cite655†CSS declaration block object is created, then:\nL1333:   1. Let `owner node` be the cite606†owner node .\nL1334:   2. If `owner node` is null, or the cite600†computed flag is set, then return.\nL1335:   3. Let `value` be the result of cite696†getting an attribute†dom.spec.whatwg.org given null, \"`style`\", and `owner node`.\nL1336:   4. If `value` is not null, let the cite602†declarations be the result of cite541†parse a CSS declaration block from a string `value`.\nL1337: \nL1338: To update style attribute for `declaration block` means to run the steps below:\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nCSS Syntax Module Level 3 (https://drafts.csswg.org/css-syntax-3/)\nciteturn1190view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"https://drafts.csswg.org/css-syntax-3/\",\"pattern\":\"To parse a list of declarations\"}); Total lines: 2053\nL915:   * \"cite279†Parse a list of declarations \" is intended for parsing anything that expects just a sequence of declaration, such as the HTML `cite280†style†html.spec.whatwg.org ` attribute, and APIs such as `cite281†the CSSStyleDeclaration cssText attribute `.\nL916:   * \"cite282†Parse a component value \" is for things that need to consume a single value, like the parsing rules for cite283†attr() .\nL917:   * \"cite284†Parse a list of component values \" is for the contents of presentational attributes, which parse text into a single declaration’s value, or for parsing a stand-alone selector cite164†[SELECT] or list of Media Queries cite285†[MEDIAQ] , as in cite286†Selectors API†www.w3.org or the `media` HTML attribute.\nL918: #### 5.4.1. Parse something according to a CSS grammar\nL919: \nL920: It is often desirable to parse a string or token list to see if it matches some CSS grammar, and if it does, to destructure it according to the grammar. This section provides a generic hook for this kind of operation. It should be invoked like \"parse `foo` as a CSS cite287†<color> \", or similar.\nL923: Note: This algorithm, and cite289†parse a comma-separated list according to a CSS grammar , are usually the only parsing algorithms other specs will want to call. The remaining parsing algorithms are meant mostly for cite134†[CSSOM] and related \"explicitly constructing CSS structures\" cases. Consult the CSSWG for guidance first if you think you need to use one of the other algorithms.\nL924: \nL925: To parse something according to a CSS grammar (aka simply cite290†parse ) given an `input` and a CSS `grammar` production:\nL926:   1. cite291†Normalize `input`, and set `input` to the result.\nL927:   2. cite284†Parse a list of component values from `input`, and let `result` be the return value.\nL928:   3. Attempt to match `result` against `grammar`. If this is successful, return the matched result; otherwise, return failure.\nL929: #### 5.4.2. Parse a comma-separated list according to a CSS grammar\nL930: \nL931: While one can definitely cite290†parse a value according to a grammar with commas in it, if any part of the value fails to parse, the entire thing doesn’t parse, and returns failure.\nL932: Sometimes that’s what’s desired (such as in list-valued CSS properties); other times, it’s better to let each comma-separated sub-part of the value parse separately, dealing with the parts that parse successfully one way, and the parts that fail to parse another way (typically ignoring them, such as in `cite292†<img sizes>†html.spec.whatwg.org `).\nL933: This algorithm provides an easy hook to accomplish exactly that. It returns a list of values split by \"top-level\" commas, where each values is either failure (if it failed to parse) or the result of parsing (an unspecified structure, as described in the cite290†parse algorithm).\nL934: \nL935: To parse a comma-separated list according to a CSS grammar (aka cite289†parse a list ) given an `input` and a CSS `grammar` production:\nL936:   1. cite291†Normalize `input`, and set `input` to the result.\nL937:   2. If `input` contains only cite166†<whitespace-token> s, return an empty cite259†list†infra.spec.whatwg.org .\nL938:   3. cite293†Parse a comma-separated list of component values from `input`, and let `list` be the return value.\nL939:   4. cite294†For each†infra.spec.whatwg.org `item` of `list`, replace `item` with the result of cite290†parsing `item` with `grammar`.\nL940:   5. Return `list`.\nL941: #### 5.4.3. Parse a stylesheet\nL942: \nL943: To parse a stylesheet from an `input` given an optional cite295†url†url.spec.whatwg.org `location`:\nL944:   1. If `input` is a byte stream for a stylesheet, cite296†decode bytes from `input`, and set `input` to the result.\nL945:   2. cite291†Normalize `input`, and set `input` to the result.\nL946:   3. Create a new stylesheet, with its cite297†location set to `location` (or null, if `location` was not passed).\nL947:   4. cite298†Consume a stylesheet’s contents from `input`, and set the stylesheet’s rules to the result.\nL948: \nL949: Note: cite298†Consume a stylesheet’s contents always consumes the entire stream.\nL950: \nL951:   5. Return the stylesheet.\nL952: #### 5.4.4. Parse a stylesheet’s contents\nL953: \nL954: To parse a stylesheet’s contents from `input`:\nL955: \nL956:   1. cite291†Normalize `input`, and set `input` to the result.\nL957:   2. cite298†Consume a stylesheet’s contents from `input`, and return the result.\nL958: \nL959: Note: cite298†Consume a stylesheet’s contents always consumes the entire stream.\nL960: #### 5.4.5. Parse a list of declarations\nL961: \nL962: To parse a list of declarations from `input`:\nL963: \nL964:   1. cite291†Normalize `input`, and set `input` to the result.\nL965:   2. Let `decls` be an empty cite259†list†infra.spec.whatwg.org .\nL966:   3. While `input` is not cite264†empty :\nL967: \nL968: cite299†Discard whitespace from `input`.\nL969: \nL970:      * If the cite265†next token from `input` is a cite130†<semicolon-token> , cite268†discard a token from `input`.\nL971:      * Otherwise, if the cite265†next token from `input` is an cite129†<at-keyword-token> , cite300†consume an at-rule from `input` and discard the result.\nL972: \nL973:      * Otherwise, cite263†consume a declaration . If anything was returned, cite301†append†infra.spec.whatwg.org it to `decls`.\nL974: \nL975:   4. Return `decls`.\nL976: \nL977: Note: The entire stream is consumed by this algorithm.\nL978: #### 5.4.6. Parse a rule\nL979: \nL980: To parse a rule from `input`:\nL981: \nL982:   1. cite291†Normalize `input`, and set `input` to the result.\nL983:   2. cite299†Discard whitespace from `input`.\nL984:   3. If `input` is cite264†empty , return a syntax error.\nL985: \nL986: Otherwise, if the cite265†next token from `input` is an cite129†<at-keyword-token> , cite300†consume an at-rule from `input`, and let `rule` be the return value.\nL991: #### 5.4.7. Parse a declaration\nL992: \nL993: To parse a declaration from `input`:\nL994: \nL995:   1. cite291†Normalize `input`, and set `input` to the result.\nL996:   2. cite299†Discard whitespace from `input`.\nL997:   3. If `input` is cite264†empty , return a syntax error.\nL998:   4. cite263†Consume a declaration from `input`. If anything was returned, let `value` be the return value. Otherwise, return a syntax error.\nL999:   5. cite299†Discard whitespace from `input`.\nL1000:   6. If `input` is cite264†empty , return `value`. Otherwise, return a syntax error.\nL1001: #### 5.4.8. Parse a component value\nL1002: \nL1003: To parse a component value from `input`:\nL1004: \nL1005:   1. cite291†Normalize `input`, and set `input` to the result.\nL1006:   2. cite299†Discard whitespace from `input`.\nL1007:   3. If `input` is cite264†empty , return a syntax error.\nL1008:   4. cite304†Consume a component value from `input` and let `value` be the return value.\nL1009:   5. cite299†Discard whitespace from `input`.\nL1010:   6. If `input` is cite264†empty , return `value`. Otherwise, return a syntax error.\nL1011: #### 5.4.9. Parse a list of component values\nL1012: \nL1013: To parse a list of component values from `input`:\nL1014: \nL1015:   1. cite291†Normalize `input`, and set `input` to the result.\nL1016: \nL1017:   2. cite305†Consume a list of component values from `input`, and return the result.\nL1018: \nL1019: Note: The entire stream is consumed by this algorithm.\nL1020: #### 5.4.10. Parse a comma-separated list of component values\nL1021: \nL1022: To parse a comma-separated list of component values from `input`:\nL1023: \nL1024:   1. cite291†Normalize `input`, and set `input` to the result.\nL1025: \nL1026:   2. Let `groups` be an empty cite259†list†infra.spec.whatwg.org .\nL1027: \nL1028:   3. While `input` is not cite264†empty :\nL1029: \nL1030:     1. cite305†Consume a list of component values from `input`, with cite200†<comma-token> as the stop token, and append the result to `groups`.\nL1031: \nL1032:     2. cite268†Discard a token from `input`.\nL1033: \nL1034:   4. Return `groups`.\n"
